Two consecutive prod auto-deploys (runs 29575860816 and 29580707533) died identically: the us-east-1 `ix --admin image push-manifest` leg of `registry.ix.dev/ix/base:latest` hung with zero progress for 45 minutes and was killed with exit 124, while the us-west-1 leg published the same manifest fine both times.

Live fleet forensics found the failure well below the deploy pipeline. On vin-compute-1, `ix-cas-fabric-server@193` had been in a systemd start-timeout crash loop since 2026-07-17 01:35:39Z, 281+ restarts deep. Each attempt burned about 1min52s of CPU over 2min7s of wall time, read 550 to 614 GB from disk, peaked at 60 to 83G of memory, and was killed at `TimeoutStartSec=120s`. At 04:00:34Z `ix-cas-fabric-handoff` reported "no active cas-fabric-server instance to rotate", so the region had no fabric backend at all. Pushes opened their RPC stream to ix-server (journal timestamps match the push start to the second) and then hung forever.

Root cause: `SegmentStore::open` in cas_disk runs a torn-tail CRC scan of every writer's active segment inline, before the unit reports `READY=1`. Sixteen writer shards times `SEGMENT_TARGET_BYTES` is 32 GiB of segment tail per open, and the leader's `.active` hint set totaled 435 GB. The 120s budget was sized when stores were under 1 GB; /proc forensics showed the single-threaded scan walking segments sequentially and dying partway through, every time.

Issue ix#7620 carries the evidence chain plus the human runbook (a runtime `TimeoutStartSec=45min` override on vin-compute-1), and PR ix#7621 raises the rendered budget to 45 minutes and fixes the stale sizing comments. Root fixes are tracked separately: parallel scans, an `EXTEND_TIMEOUT_USEC` heartbeat during the scan, and a clean-shutdown seal marker so orderly stops skip the rescan entirely. This is a distinct root cause from ix#6165, whose pushes died mid-stream on transport errors; these never received a byte.

